Ever wondered why some files in Linux start with a dot (.)?

In Linux, any file beginning with a . is a hidden file — often used for configurations, environment settings, and system preferences. 

👉 Example:

ls -a

This command reveals all files, including hidden ones like .bashrc, .gitignore, or .ssh/.

Dotfiles may look small, but they hold the power to customize your Linux experience — from aliases to shell behavior! 

Every DevOps engineer should know: .files = hidden secrets of Linux productivity. 
